**/.DS_Store
.venv/
.envrc.local
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
staging/
my_models/reference/
dev/
bnf_*.log
# PyBNF console capture from a driven campaign. Same artefact as bnf_*.log above -- the
# overnight/weekend drivers redirect to .out, and the .log-only rule let 99 of these (2.3 GB,
# one of them 969 MB) sit untracked in the tree until a sync. A run's console is never a
# deliverable; what ships is copied out as best_fit_params.txt / information_criteria.txt.
bnf_*.out
**/results_*/

# PyBNF run output. A fit writes output_dir/ (Results/, FailedSimLogs/, alg_backup.bp) into the
# job directory it runs in, so a broad `git add <slug-dir>` while a fit is in flight sweeps a
# scratch run into the tree -- which is exactly how 15 such files reached 7e3a63b. Shipped fit
# provenance is copied OUT of a run as best_fit_params.txt / information_criteria.txt; the run
# directory itself is never a deliverable.
**/output/
**/output_*/

# Local checkout of the upstream PEtab benchmark collection, kept beside the jobs for
# regenerating nominal checks. Deliberately not vendored -- see Grein-2026-benchmark-subset-I's
# README ("not one upstream PEtab problem file lives here") and upstream.json, which pins the
# commit instead.
pybnf-jobs/Benchmark-Models-PEtab/

# Driven-campaign fan-out (issue #38: Borghans, Elowitz). The rules above cover what PyBNF
# itself writes -- output_*/, Results/, the console capture. A *driven* campaign also writes
# its own inputs and its own bookkeeping, and those land in the job directory beside the run
# directories rather than inside them, so nothing above described them. That gap is how 326
# generated files accumulated untracked across six days of runs.
#
# What is ignored here is strictly machine output: one config per run, expanded by a driver
# from a committed template, plus the status tables and progress logs the drivers append to.
# The drivers and their templates are committed -- they are the reproducible part, and a
# generated config is recoverable by re-running the driver that emitted it. Start-point JSONs
# regenerate exactly: make_radius_starts.py and find_oscillating_starts.py both default to
# --seed 20260814 through np.random.default_rng.
#
# Deliberately narrow. Each pattern names the arm that produced it, so a new hand-written
# template is NOT swept up by an existing rule -- Borghans_bench_a2_cmaes_gntr.conf is a
# template and stays tracked, while Borghans_bench_a2_s1_n01_seed42.conf is a run and does not.

# Borghans: per-run configs expanded by run_overnight_campaign.py / run_paired_oscillating.py
Borghans_overnight_s*_n*_seed*.conf
Borghans_bench_a?_s*_n*_seed*.conf
Borghans_nom[0-9][0-9]_[gm]*.conf
Borghans_osc[0-9][0-9]_[gm]*.conf
Borghans_probe[0-9]*_[gm]*.conf
Borghans_proto[0-9]*_[gm]*.conf
Borghans_proto[0-9]*_dev_ms.conf
Borghans_rad[0-9]*_[gm]*.conf
Borghans_rad_b[0-9]*_[gm]*.conf

# Elowitz: per-run configs expanded by weekend_ms_vs_gntr.sh / run_arms.sh / calibrate_box.sh
Elowitz_wk_gntr_s*.conf
Elowitz_wk_ms_s*.conf
Elowitz_cal_d*_s*.conf
Elowitz_pilot_a*.conf
Elowitz_run_a*.conf

# Campaign bookkeeping: status tables the drivers append a row to, and their progress logs
*_status.tsv
wk_lane?.tsv
wk_lane?.log
*_progress.log
*_stderr.log
arms_pilot.log
arms_run.log
calibration.log
overnight_deadline_guard.log
proto[0-9]*.log
proto_reproduce*.log

# Generated start points and scan results (regenerable; see the seed note above)
_alpha_nominal.json
_chunk_[0-9]*.json
_nominal_for_alpha.json
_nominal_start.json
alpha_scan_results.json
oscillating_starts.json
prototype_seed[0-9]*_start.json
radius_starts*.json
